@node Windowed Computations
@section Windowed computations

The upstream book leaves this chapter as a title.  The building blocks
for windowed computations are already in libdtfl, so this section
sketches how they fit together rather than porting prose that does not
exist.

A window is a bound on which times contribute to an output.  Two
operators express it.  @code{dtfl_collection_delay} re-times each update
by a monotone function, which can round a record's time up to the end of
the window it falls in, so all records of a window share one output
time.  Advancing the input past that time, with
@code{dtfl_input_session_advance_to}, then lets the window's output
finalize while later windows are still open; a probe reports exactly
which windows are settled.  For a sliding window, @code{explode} can
place one record into several windows by emitting it once per window
with the appropriate factor.  Combined with @code{reduce} or
@code{count} keyed by window, these give the windowed aggregates a
streaming computation usually wants, maintained incrementally like every
other differential collection.
